Z||000012||Slammed Fiero||07-16-2001||03:43 PM|||| <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Archie:<BR><B>  <BR>BTW, before someone says that $260 is too much for the adapters, let me assure you that  YOU WANT THESE THINGS MADE CORRECTLY... Your life depends on it. Think about that when you're hacking away at making a set in your garage next weekend.   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"><P>Hope this helps<P>Archie<P><BR>[This message has been edited by Archie (edited 07-15-2001).]</B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>I just did the identical brake conversion $260 is a Good price , I paid $500 Canadian for a machine shop to make my adaptors. you don't want to screw around when it comes to braking.<P>Archie Do you sell those braided hoses seperatly?? I'm looking ofr a set of correct length braided units <P>Thanks!<P><P>------------------<BR>Jonathan McCreery<BR>86 GT 4spd<BR>Koni's , Coilovers, Big Bars , 17" Trmotorsports Typhoons , 11.25" vented discs, Wilwood 4 Piston Calipers , Pirelli P Zero's, Short Shift, No cat , Cold air induction , Geni Stainless tips, White face gauges , Momo shift , momo pedals, White face Gauges , 70Hp Shot of NOS Autocrossed , Tracked , Dragged? (soon) abused.||||reg||1
Z||000013||Pontiaddict||07-16-2001||04:41 PM||||My 86 Trans Am had the Rear disk brake option. It's almost exactly the same as the Seville setup. I don't like the cast iron calipers though and if it were me I'd like to find a brake kit that used the later style PBR calipers even if it meant using 16" or larger wheels.||||reg||1
Z||000014||Patrick's Dad||07-16-2001||07:49 PM||||[SaleenFiero Quote] Crap, Ceramic rotors would be through the roof price wise! Aren't they like a $10k upgrade on the 911? [/Quote]<P>I'd heard that they were a $5K upgrade on a Benz.<P> <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR> Why the hell would a Fiero need ceramics? (Although it would be damn cool to see!) <HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>For the same reasons on any other car; no warpage, better transfer of heat, lower unsprung weight. And they're cool looking.<P>They're not likely in my future, but it's fun to muse about the possibilities.||||reg||1
Z||000015||Archie||07-16-2001||08:49 PM||||Ok, let's see if I can answer most of the questions in this one message.<P>1) ceramic rotors?<BR>Dunno, I guess, Why?<P>2) why no E-brake with Camaro calipers?<BR>Actually many Camaros had rear discs, however the Caddies are easier to find at a parts store, they are cheaper & the "E" brake lever is a little better for the Fiero.<P>3)  Wildwood calipers..... Your opinion?<BR>IMHO, either you want an exotic braking system where cost is no object or you want a low cost system that has easy to get parts and stops just as well. I've had several of the Ryanne systems and a few of the G.A. systems & several of these set-ups. I like these.<P>4) How much for the complete kit Archie?<BR>Including machining your rotors & the Caddie calipers $1450.<P>5) how much for you to machine the rotors?<BR>$70 My machine shop charges me $60 plus $10 for shipping to you.<P>I hope this helps.<P>Archie<P>||||reg||1
Z||000016||TI_3VOM||07-17-2001||12:37 AM||||Archie...couple of quick questions;<BR>1) Is your set-up only for the 84-87 Fiero, or can it also be mounted on the 88?<BR>2) Using the parts listed, what (if any) increase/decrease is there in unsprung weight?<BR>||||reg||5
